#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# glow

#% text
# `glow(c)` is a directive of 3D graphics: light of colour `c` given off by the faces that follow.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The 3D renderer shades faces with its own fixed light; `glow` is accepted in graphics and plot options, so code written for other lighting runs, and is not drawn.

#% item
# The call stays as it is when evaluated alone.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
graphics3d([glow(yellow), sphere()])
#% output
#= image("glow.files/glow-1.svgz")

#% cell
plot3d(sin(x)*cos(y), [x, 0, 3], [y, 0, 3], plot_style: [glow(red)])
#% output
#= image("glow.files/glow-2.svgz")

#% cell
glow(red)
#% output
#= glow(red)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The picture is the same without it:

#% cell
graphics3d([orange, sphere()])
#% output
#= image("glow.files/glow-4.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `graphics3d`, `plot3d`, `lighting`, `specularity`
